# Session sharing settings for this build (read by run.sh, so it covers run-openai.sh too).
#
# SYNC_CLAUDE_SESSIONS=1 -> the session store is SHARED with Claude Desktop, as a symlink:
#
#   <repo>/user-data/claude-code-sessions
#     -> ~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude-code-sessions
#
# Each local_<uuid>.json is one session's metadata — sessionId, cliSessionId, cwd, title,
# titleSource, isArchived, model, effort, permissionMode, enabledMcpTools, lastActivityAt.
#
# This used to be a one-way `rsync --update` copy, which is why issue #3 existed: sessions
# created or renamed HERE had nowhere to go, so the two stores drifted apart in both
# directions. By the time it was changed there were 13 sessions only in the real install,
# 64 only in this build, and 15 that differed. Sharing removes the whole problem — there is
# one directory, so nothing to copy, nothing to merge, and nothing to go stale.
#
# Safe to share because these are plain per-session JSON files: no lock, no database, and
# both sides already use the identical <user>/<org> path so the link needs no translation.
# Local Storage canNOT be shared the same way — see SYNC_CLAUDE_UI_STATE below for why.
#
# TWO CONSEQUENCES, both deliberate:
#
#  1. This build now WRITES into the real install's data. Everything else in user-data/ is
#     still isolated; this one directory is not. Verified in both directions: a file created
#     under user-data/claude-code-sessions/ appears at the real install path and vice versa.
#
#  2. Deletion is GLOBAL. `isArchived` is 0 across every session file on both sides, so the
#     UI removes a session by deleting its file — and it is now one file, not two. Removing
#     a session in either app removes it from both.
#
# Before the symlink can be created, anything unique to this build has to be merged into the
# shared store, or it would be abandoned. run.sh refuses to link while that is true and tells
# you to run `node scripts/merge-sessions.mjs` (use --dry-run first; it backs both trees up
# to user-data/claude-code-sessions.premerge and prints a per-file decision table).
#
# NOT shared, because it does not need to be: the agent's config and memory are ALREADY
# common. Both apps resolve CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR to $HOME/.claude, so this build reads the
# same ~/.claude/projects/*/memory/*.md, MEMORY.md, CLAUDE.md, settings and session
# transcripts. Verified: a session created in this build wrote its transcript to
# ~/.claude/projects/<slug>/<cliSessionId>.jsonl, and its child processes carry
# HOME=$HOME with no CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR override.
#
# Set to 0 to launch without touching the session store at all. An existing symlink is left
# in place — remove it by hand if you want the profiles separate again, and note that doing
# so leaves this build with NO sessions until you copy some back.
SYNC_CLAUDE_SESSIONS=1

# SYNC_CLAUDE_UI_STATE -> copy claude.ai's Local Storage from Claude Desktop.
#
# WHY THIS IS A COPY AND NOT A SYMLINK, unlike the sessions above. LevelDB allows exactly
# one process at a time. Both databases hold an exclusive whole-file fcntl(F_WRLCK) on their
# LOCK file for as long as their app runs — verified directly with F_GETLK:
#
#   .../Claude/Local Storage/leveldb/LOCK      F_WRLCK  (Claude Desktop)
#   .../user-data/Local Storage/leveldb/LOCK   F_WRLCK  (this build)
#
# Point both apps at one directory and the second to start cannot open its Local Storage at
# all. A native LevelDB binding does not help: it takes the same lock on the same inode.
# Reads are unaffected (parsing the files never touches LOCK), so the only shapes available
# for this store are pull-before-launch and push-after-quit.
#
# DEFAULT 0, and the reason matters. This was added to make session GROUPING sync, on the
# evidence that Local Storage holds LSS-persisted.dframe-group-scopes. Capturing the network
# with --net-log-capture-mode=Everything then showed that is only a local MIRROR: the real
# store is server-side at
#   GET /api/claude_code/organizations/{org}/user_settings
# which returns groupByByMode, customGroups (id + name), customGroupAssignments and
# customGroupOrder. Because it follows the account, both apps already see identical grouping
# and there is nothing to sync. Copying Local Storage therefore cannot help grouping, while it
# does replace this build's own composer drafts and sidebar prefs — so it is off by default.
#
# Set to 1 only if you specifically want this build's claude.ai UI state replaced by Claude
# Desktop's. The guards still apply: skipped while Claude Desktop is running, previous state
# backed up to "user-data/Local Storage.bak", and the copy verified before being kept.
#
# Unlike the session sync this is a whole-directory copy and it REPLACES this build's own
# claude.ai UI state (composer drafts, sidebar prefs). The previous state is kept at
# "user-data/Local Storage.bak". It is skipped entirely while Claude Desktop is running,
# because copying an open LevelDB can capture a torn write, and the result is verified to
# contain the grouping keys before being kept.
#
# WARNING, learned the hard way: replacing or deleting Local Storage DOES break login. An
# earlier version of this comment claimed it did not, on the reasoning that authentication lives
# in Cookies. Deleting Local Storage produced 401s and a failed bootstrap until the directory was
# restored, so the desktop keeps auth state there too.
#
# Also: never restore a LevelDB backup taken from a profile that was not closed cleanly. A backup
# copied after the app was killed with pkill left the app hanging in window setup — renderers
# reporting "Terminating current process after 15 seconds with no connection" — because the copy
# captured a torn write. The recovery is to copy a known-good Local Storage in, not to delete it.
SYNC_CLAUDE_UI_STATE=0
